From b2e0e5edf9b3bf540e89428b297df8d10a907410 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christian F.K. Schaller" <christian.schaller@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:15:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] scanner: Show the dialog before the search for scanners is
 over

This commit makes SelectScanners show the dialog after at most 300
milliseconds with the scanners found by then, and send the others as
they are found. The dialog used to wait until libsane had asked every
driver, one after the other. That took about 6 seconds with network
scanners on the desktop this was tried on, nearly all of it spent
waiting for the network, while a scanner on USB is found in
milliseconds.

The helper now runs once per driver, as many as 12 at a time, each
with a configuration directory that loads that driver alone, with the
driver's own files linked in. The drivers are the ones dll.conf and
dll.d name in the directories SANE_CONFIG_DIR gives, or in
/etc/sane.d. The search takes as long as the slowest driver, and the
ids of the scanners do not change. Without a dll.conf the helper runs
once, as before.

The search runs in a thread. SelectScanners waits for it for 300
milliseconds, then calls the backend as before, with `searching` set in
the options if the search goes on. What is found later goes to the
backend in the new UpdateScanners call, which ends with one that says
the search is over. Only scanners that were offered to the dialog can
be granted, and those found late are offered when they are found. A
search that ends fills the list that ListScanners and OpenScanner use.

The backend interface gets UpdateScanners and the `searching` option;
the documentation says what a dialog is to do with them. The index of
the documentation now lists the pages of the scanner portal, which it
did not, and which the build warned about.

Measured with the system's SANE configuration, listing through the
frontend took 1.7 seconds, down from about 6, with the same three
scanners and ids. With a listing that is made to take 2 seconds,
SelectScanners returned in 0.30.

Tested on 1.22.1: the scenario checks pass 46 of 46, among them three
for a search that takes longer than the dialog waits. The GNOME and the
GTK backends took the scanners found late and the end of the search on
a private bus.
